      <h2>What goes in each issue</h2>

      <h3>1. Tip</h3>
      <p>One specific, useful observation drawn from a recent audit, build, or post, written in two to four plain-language sentences. The goal is to teach something concrete the reader can use without me, whether or not they ever hire me.</p>

      <h3>2. Ship</h3>
      <p>One thing I shipped recently, whether a new blog post, a new sample site, an updated service page, or a public changelog entry. One or two sentences plus a link, mostly to demonstrate that the agency is active and improving.</p>

      <h3>3. Ask</h3>
      <p>One soft call to action, and not the same one every time. Sometimes it is "got a friend in [trade] still on Wix? Forward this." Sometimes it is "if your own site is on the list of things to fix, the free audit is open." Never a hard pitch.</p>

      <h2>Why bi-weekly</h2>
      <p>Weekly is too much for me to write at the standard I want, and monthly is too thin to stay top-of-mind. Bi-weekly is the cadence that produces real signal without filler, and if I do not have something genuinely useful in a given week, I skip it rather than fill the page.</p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>The list runs on <a href="https://buttondown.com" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">Buttondown</a>, an indie, privacy-first email platform. Your email is used to send you the newsletter and nothing else, with no tracking pixels and no third-party retargeting. Unsubscribing takes one click, and the link is in every issue. The full <a href="/privacy-policy/" class="inline-link">privacy policy</a> covers the rest.</p>

      <h2>Archive</h2>
      <p>Past issues will appear on this page once the third one is live. Until then, the recent <a href="/blog/" class="inline-link">blog posts</a> cover the same territory in long form.</p>
